WEBVTT

NOTE
Automatic English captions. Generated locally and checked with additional audio decodes; not fully audited by listening.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.060
The agent block lets you call a language
model, but most useful agents don't just

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:10.980
answer a question and then stop. They need
to look things up, call services, check

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:17.100
what came back, decide what to do next.
Tool calling is what gives an agent that

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:21.880
loop, a way to choose actions, get
feedback from the world, and keep going

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:26.120
until it has enough to return something
useful. Let me show you how it works.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:31.900
Here's a plain agent block with a simple
prompt and no tools. Text goes in, the

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:36.520
model runs, and text comes out. It can't
really qualify the lead the way we want

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:40.820
because it lacks the context it needs. In
the worst case, it might even hallucinate

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:46.780
a response. We can give the agent some
tools, and now it works in a loop. Each

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:51.040
turn, the model decides whether it needs a
tool and which one, and it can even call

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:56.120
several at once. It reads what comes back,
revises its plan, and calls again with

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:57.120
different arguments.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:05.100
Every tool call gets added to its context,
and each step builds on the last. The

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:08.200
agent repeatedly calls tools to converge
towards a solution.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:14.060
Once the agent decides it's done, it
synthesizes an answer from the context

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:19.320
accumulated from all the tool calls. You
can add tools to an agent block in the

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:23.480
tools section of the block's parameters.
Any block you'd normally bring as a

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:28.440
workflow step can be a tool. When
selecting tools for an agent block, you

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:32.980
can fill in block parameters to fix their
values. The model will fill in the tool

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:38.340
arguments you leave empty intelligently
based on the task. Sometimes a model might

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:43.860
not choose to use a tool, but you can
guarantee it does by choosing force. If no

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:48.220
block does quite what you need, you can
write your own tool. You give it a schema

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:52.300
and a description, and that description is
how the model knows when to reach for it.

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:58.520
You can also connect a whole external tool
set at once over MCP. You can see exactly

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:03.800
which tools the agent decided to use by
reading back the run. The content field is

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:09.380
the model's final answer, written after it
worked through all the tools. Open up the

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:12.180
tool calls, and you get every call it made
in order.

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:16.200
Each one shows the name of the tool it
called.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:20.620
The arguments the model filled in to
invoke it.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:26.320
And the result that came back into its
context.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:32.900
The calls are kept in order, so you can
trace each step and see why the agent

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:34.000
answered the way it did.
